# SpeakesQuery is a local-trust desktop app - there is no multi-user
# login system.  A single access token (the Jupyter model) gates the
# server whenever it binds beyond loopback: Docker installs are gated
# automatically and install.sh prints the ready-to-open ?token= URL.
# Loopback bare-metal runs and the PyWebView desktop app stay
# token-free.  All settings below are optional; the only common reason
# to populate this file is SMTP for email delivery of scheduled-search
# alerts.  Everything else is set in-app on the Settings page or via
# global_settings.yaml.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Precedence - why every line below is commented out by default
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both the Docker image (via `env_file:` in desktop_app/docker-compose.yml)
# and PyCharm's default Python run config load this file into the
# process environment at startup.  Env vars WIN over values saved on
# the Settings page - so if you uncomment a line here, it will override
# whatever you configure in the UI.  For most users the right choice is
# to leave every SMTP_* line commented out and configure email through
# Settings → Email instead; that way the UI is the single source of
# truth and `.env` stays out of the auth path entirely.
#
# Known placeholder values (`you@gmail.com`, `your_16_char_app_password`,
# etc.) are auto-detected and ignored with a one-shot WARN line - so a
# stock `.env` copied verbatim by install.sh cannot silently poison
# AUTH even if someone forgets to edit it.  Real credentials pasted in
# place of the placeholders are used normally.

# ── Email / SMTP (optional - leave commented out to use Settings page) ──
# For Gmail: use an App Password (https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords)
# Uncomment AND replace the placeholder values to use env-var config:
#
# SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
# SMTP_PASSWORD=your_16_char_app_password
# SMTP_SERVER=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_FROM=              # defaults to SMTP_USER
# SMTP_STARTTLS=true      # set to false for port 465 (implicit SSL)

# ── Anthropic Claude API (optional, for the analyzer + alert groups) ──
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# ── Allowed API domains for sandboxed ingestion scripts (optional) ──
# Comma-separated regex patterns.  When unset, the per-task setting from
# global_settings.yaml's `allowed_api_domains` is used instead.
# ALLOWED_API_DOMAINS=^api\.example\.com$,^data\.example\.com$

# ── Network exposure (Docker only) ──
# The Docker host port mapping binds to 127.0.0.1 by default: a fresh
# install is reachable ONLY from the machine it runs on. To reach the
# app from other devices on your network, uncomment BIND_ADDR and read
# the "Exposing beyond localhost" section of
# docs/lang/06_application_guide.md FIRST - the app ships without
# multi-user access controls, so LAN exposure is an explicit,
# eyes-open opt-in.
#
# BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0     # or a specific interface IP, e.g. 192.168.1.10
# PORT=5111             # host port (also settable via ./install.sh --port)

# ── Access-token gate (W11b, the Jupyter model) ──
# Default behavior needs NO configuration: the gate turns on exactly
# when the server binds beyond loopback (all Docker installs; any
# HOST override on bare metal) and stays off for localhost runs. The
# token lives at ~/.speakes-query/access_token (0600, outside the
# repo); install.sh generates it and prints the ?token= URL.
#
# SPEAKESQUERY_AUTH=on          # force the gate on even for loopback
# SPEAKESQUERY_AUTH=off         # disable - ONLY behind a reverse proxy
#                               # that enforces its own auth
# SPEAKESQUERY_ACCESS_TOKEN=    # override the token file (e.g. secret
#                               # mounts in orchestrated deployments)
